      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you slice an onion, the enzyme alliinase reacts with the substrate S-alk(en)yl-l-cysteine sulfoxide, which was previously separated by the plant's cell walls. This enzymatic pathway rapidly converts the substrate into syn-propanethial-S-oxide, a highly volatile gas that travels up to your eyes. Upon reaching the ocular surface, this gas reacts with water in the tear film to form sulfuric and sulfurous acids. These acids then trigger sensory nerves in the eyes, causing the stinging, tearing, and redness you experience. You can emphasize that while the initial cleavage is enzymatic, the actual irritation comes from the subsequent acid-base reaction with moisture.",
